@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "Dakar 1984";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

<div class="text-center">
	<h1>
		Summary (1984 - Paris - Algiers - Dakar)
	</h1>
	<p>
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1984/1984_map.jpg" style="float: left; height: 117px" />Once
		again, the 1984 Paris - Algiers - Dakar presented a fascinating challenge,
		this time with 11,000 km of the toughest terrain to be found anywhere in the world.
		The compact, agile and rugged Mitsubishi Pajero, prepared and entered
		by the French importer Sonauto, this time started in the class for minimal
		modifications to engine and suspension for "Marathon Man" Andrew Cowan. Although
		the Pajero, at 2 years old, was one of the youngest cars, no f<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1984/1984_01.jpg" style="float: right; height: 86px" />ewer
		than 11 team crews chose it, still impressed by its debut the year before. From
		the Place de la Concorde in Paris, the Pajero teams set out with high hopes
		and crossed the Mediterranean towards Algeria, from where the rally convoy
		set off with a loud howl into the depths of the Nigerien desert. The
		unloved and feared Tenere desert in the east of the Sahara, with its sandstorms
		and forces of nature, was to play a major role. Strangely enough<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1984/1984_02.jpg"
																	   style="float: left; height: 100px" />, Cowan's Pajero never caused any problems.
		Mitsubishi's legendary reliability had carried over superbly to the Pajero,
		and despite its age of just 2 years, it had few problems coping with
		the toughest engine demands in the world. Even against highly tuned,
		purpose-built desert racers, the mildly modified Pajero held its own.
		Having emerged as winner of its own class, Cowan drove to third place
		in the overall standings, beating many of the desert racers, such as Jacky
		Ickx's Porsche. It was a great moment for Cowan and his Mitsubishi Pajero,
		which in its second year of Dakar participation earned the reputation of being the greatest all-terrain
		machine in cross-country competition.
	</p>
